<section id="limit-cycles" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="limit-cycles">Limit Cycles</h2>
<p><strong>Definition</strong></p>
<p>A limit cycle is an <em>isolated</em> closed trajectory in phase space.</p>
<p><strong>Brief Description</strong></p>
<p>Recall that a closed trajectory in the phase space of a dynamical system represents a periodic solution. So a limit cycle describes a periodic solution that is isolated from all other periodic solutions - i.e.&nbsp;there are no other closed trajectories in a small neighbourhood around a limit cycle.</p>
<p>Limit cycles only exist in <em>nonlinear</em> systems. If <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cpmb%7Bx%7D(t)"> is a periodic solution to a linear system <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Cdot%7B%5Cpmb%7Bx%7D%7D=A%5Cpmb%7Bx%7D">, then <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?c%5Cpmb%7Bx%7D(t)"> is also a solution for any constant <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?c%5Cin%5Cmathbb%7BR%7D">. So for any linear system, either no closed trajectories exist, or an infinite family of closed trajectories exist.</p>
<p>A limit cycle is said to be <em>stable</em> if all neighbouring trajectories converge to the limit cycle, and <em>unstable</em> if all neighbouring trajectories diverge from the limit cycle. <em>Half-stable</em> limit cycles exist as well, but are difficult to generalize to phase spaces of dimension <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?3"> or higher. In the phase plane, a half-stable limit cycle can be understood as a limit cycle that is stable for trajectories on the interior of the limit cycle, and unstable for trajectories on the exterior (or <em>vice versa</em>).</p>
</section>
